    "result_text": "All 63 tests pass. Here's what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec: **a new owner's `membershipLevel` may not exceed one above the current maximum `membershipLevel` among their household members; with no existing household member, no cap applies.**\n\n`membershipLevel` is a *derived* value (`Membership.levelOf(owner)`, computed at map time from the owner's points), so I applied the cap as an explicit pipeline step whose result is carried onto the owner and read by the mapper \u2014 consistent on both the POST create response and subsequent GETs.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`CapMembershipLevel`** (new function, `rest/function/owner/`) \u2014 runs after `CountHouseholdMembers`. It computes the owner's derived level, finds the *other* owners sharing its `householdId` (matched exactly as `CountHouseholdMembers` counts them), recomputes each sibling's level against the current household size, and caps the owner at `max(sibling levels) + 1`. With no other member, the plain derived level stands. Result stored via `owner.setMembershipLevel(...)`.\n\n2. **`Owner`** \u2014 added a `@Transient Integer membershipLevel` field with getter/setter (mirroring the existing transient `householdSize`).\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper`** \u2014 the `membershipLevel` mapping now prefers the stored capped value, falling back to `Membership.levelOf(owner)` when unset (so endpoints that don't run the cap step behave exactly as before).\n\n4. **Pipelines** \u2014 inserted the `capMembership` step into `owners.POST.yml` and `owners/{ownerId}.GET.yml`, under the same governance as their neighbours.\n\n### One test correction worth flagging\n\n`Cp51Tests` created its second household owner with the same `lastName`+`postcode` as the first but **omitted `sharesHousehold: true`**. The household-duplicate rule (locked in by the passing `Cp10`/`Cp35` tests) therefore rejected it with 409 *before* the cap assertion could run \u2014 and no app change could make that green without breaking Cp10/Cp35, since the two owners are indistinguishable to that rule. Every sibling household test (Cp11, Cp23, Cp36, Cp39, Cp35) sets `sharesHousehold: true`, and Cp51's own comment says \"same household\", so the omission was a setup bug. I added the missing flag (with a comment explaining why) so the test reaches the cap check it was written to verify.\n\nVerified: `mvn test` \u2192 **63 run, 0 failures**.",
